# Canonicalization

Canonicalization selects one declared state from the system states that meet the same requirements and makes it the required state. Its purpose is to minimize the ongoing effort required to maintain the system. A canonical declarative system declares all state that must persist, so it can be rebuilt from its declarations instead of tracing past changes.

A canonical declarative system:

- Is defined with NixOS and uses an ephemeral root filesystem, so the machine can be rebuilt from declarations and only declared state persists.
- Keeps its home directory in a Git repository with a whitelist `.gitignore`, making the files kept in Git explicit and reviewable.
- Organizes projects at paths based on their Git remotes (for example, `github.com/owner/project`), so their locations can be discovered and checked without configuration for each project.
- Treats each project as a Git submodule and a Nix flake, allowing it to be versioned, initialized, rebuilt, and checked independently.
- Uses a defined subset of the https://github.com/numtide/blueprint directory layout so tools can create and check flake repositories: each has a README explaining its purpose, packages with a single source file, and tests named for expected behavior.

`git canonicalization` initializes flake repositories, creates their packages, and checks home and flake repositories.

Initialize the home repository:

```sh
cd
git init
printf '*\n!/.gitignore\n!/.gitmodules\n' > .gitignore # add other required paths, such as .ssh, to the whitelist
git add .gitignore
git commit -m 'Initialize home repository'
```

Add and initialize a project submodule:

```sh
# Create the empty remote before starting this workflow.
git submodule add --force git@github.com:owner/demo.git ~/github.com/owner/demo
cd ~/github.com/owner/demo
git canonicalization init . # initializes a canonical flake repository
git canonicalization add <type> <name> # creates a package in the current flake repository
nix fmt # runs standard formatters and strict linters
nix flake check # checks the layout and runs builds and tests
```
